"<meeting id=\"e8fbfc78-dc6c-448c-9ce0-d262c3316499\" title=\"Catch up w/ Alec\" date=\"Jul 28, 2026 2:37 PM GMT+1\">\n  <known_participants>\n  Kris Brown (note creator) from Humans Not Robots &lt;kris@humansnotrobots.com&gt;\n  </known_participants>\n  \n  <summary>\n### Agentic Build Architecture (Coolbox + Capability Levels)\n\n- Coolbox: Docker wrapper around Claude Code handling permissions, caging, and environment mounting\n  - Runs automated agents in a contained environment, no permission prompts, no container escape\n  - Mounts transcripts and relevant context to feel native, without exposing anything else\n- Capability levels (L1 to L4) define how much autonomy the agent has:\n  - L1: human instigates each step (spec, review, build)\n  - L2: pre-approved tickets run automated in a small batch\n  - L3: CI-triggered, always watching for new work (currently being wired up)\n  - L4: lights-out factory, given a PRD and builds the product end-to-end with full PR/linear visibility\n- Spec pipeline: ticket raised via Linear template, spec written and reviewed by models, ADRs generated and committed to repo at build time\n- Holdout agent: spun up in an isolated environment with only withheld tests, runs against a live build to validate without access to code or specs\n- Review modes: standard, adversarial, common values, and quorum (multiple model families synthesized)\n- Model selection by stage: reasoning-heavy models for spec and eval; lightweight/local models for build (no decisions required)\n- Mosh shell used for SSH over spotty connections, keeping agent sessions alive across drops\n\n### Skills, Governance, and the Human Capability Model\n\n- Skills are slash commands that modify agent behavior: some are structural, some are opinionated (agile-style, splitting PRDs into tickets)\n- Skills assigned per agent role: e.g. a spec agent gets different skills than a build agent\n- Governance skills add guardrails, especially relevant for lower-capability users (the “vibe coder” profile)\n- Human capability model (the simulator experiment):\n  - Frames task outcomes as a function of intelligence, learning ability, skills/knowledge, and governance\n  - AI amplifies trajectory in both directions: better outcomes for prepared users, worse for unprepared ones\n  - Profiles modeled: vibe coder (no governance, no learning), average trained professional, forward-thinking architect with guardrails\n  - Key insight: matching intelligence level to task complexity sets the midline; governance deflects the trajectory upward\n\n### Knowledge Islands and Tooling\n\n- Two repo types: engineering-style depot and knowledge-based repo\n- Knowledge island structure: admin, calendar, pillars, resources, and streams (work stream tracking)\n- Recap loop: on completing work, agent reviews completed items, flags outstanding tasks, captures lessons, and generates named actions pushed to roadmap\n- Handoff mechanism: outbound and inbound queues between repos, knowledge-as-file rather than work-as-message\n- Custom MCPs built to keep tool surface small; MCP Porter used so tools are discovered on demand rather than loaded into context\n- Linear MCP dropped due to context cost; managing Linear via other means\n- Live artifacts in Claude UI used as a command center: repo status dashboard, quick edits, observability at a glance\n- Legal case management built on the same knowledge island pattern: triple fact-checks, judicial intervention simulation, opposing counsel simulation\n\n### R\\&amp;D Direction and Collaboration\n\n- Plan: Kris and the other person do R\\&amp;D together, two days a week to start, with a view to increasing as bridging is in place\n- Engineering team handles execution; Kris focuses on governance, architecture, and new capability building\n- Goal: build things to sell (AI tooling/products), not just internal automation\n- Value measurement discussion: current gap is attribution of cost to value\n  - Measurement exists, concept exists, but no model yet for “what is value” or tracing AI contribution to business outcomes\n  - Sponsorship/experience-point model floated: companies set their own value targets, AI work earns points against those targets\n  - Linking into ALM (e.g. Linear) to tie completed work back to cost and value distribution seen as a promising direction\n- Gastown mentioned as a comparable: crowdsourced, RPG-style AI orchestration with tokenization layer; noted as very similar in concept to what’s being explored\n\n### Next Steps\n\n- **Increase collaboration to two days a week**\n\n  Start as soon as possible; revisit increasing commitment once bridging arrangements are in place.\n\n- **Define a value attribution model for AI work**\n\n  Bridge the gap between measurement and business objectives: explore sponsorship/XP model and ALM-linked cost attribution.\n\n- **Review Gastown in more detail**\n\n  Assess overlap with the knowledge island and value-tracking direction being explored.\n\n- **Wire L3 capability onto CI**\n\n  Currently triggered interactively; goal is for it to run automatically against new work on the box.\n</summary>\n</meeting>"
